    "narrative": "Today\u2019s slate for **Ghost Ride the WHIP** is anchored by a high-upside matchup for **Isaac Paredes**, who leads the squad with a 72.13 efficiency score. Facing George Klassen (6.12 xERA) provides an elite environment for **Isaac Paredes** to exploit his strong platoon advantage and favorable SP matchup, making him our primary engine for counting stats. **Mickey Moniak** (69.76) and **Corbin Carroll** (66.31) follow closely behind; **Mickey Moniak** benefits immensely from a +14% park factor, though he carries a 37% rain risk warning that could shorten his day. **Corbin Carroll** remains a critical start, as his ability to leverage a slow SP delivery for stolen bases\u2014our scarcest category\u2014adds significant weight to his strong baseline projection. \n\nWhile the optimizer is aggressive on power, the benching of **Josh Naylor** (44.49) was the most difficult decision of the morning. **Josh Naylor** fell just short of the cut largely due to a harsh -19% SP skill penalty against Kyle Harrison, which outweighed the marginal scoring provided by his projected #5 spot in the lineup. Conversely, **Austin Riley** (59.52) secured a start over deeper options because his matchup against Zebby Matthews is bolstered by a +7% SP skill advantage and, crucially, a +2% SB boost due to the pitcher\u2019s slow delivery. We are also monitoring **Gabriel Moreno**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Max Muncy**, **Brice Turang**, **Jose Caballero**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Henry Bolte**, and **Vinnie Pasquantino** to confirm they remain in their projected slots as lineups finalize, particularly to ensure **Seiya Suzuki** retains his +5% wind-out advantage. With **James Wood** still on the IL and other depth pieces like **Tyler Soderstrom**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Cole Young**, and **Edwin Arroyo** currently relegated to non-starting roles, we are prioritizing high-contact floor and speed potential to keep our WHIP-chasing momentum alive.",
